{
	title: 'A turn stops when it should: round cap, stop reasons, killed tools',
	description: 'A turn never spirals and never leaves a dangerous command running after the user stopped it. (1) Round cap: a turn runs at most `maxRounds` provider rounds (a config.ason setting in src/common/settings.ts, default 200; not shown in the UI beyond the pause reason); reaching it pauses the turn with a reason saying so, and Enter continues for another maxRounds. (2) Stop reasons, as the old Hal (~/.hal/src/server/providers/anthropic.ts, NORMAL_STOP_REASONS): a round that ends because of max_tokens (or context window exceeded) or a refusal runs none of its tool calls (a call cut off mid-input is not a call) and ends the turn as an error that says why ("Response stopped: max_tokens", or the refusal\'s explanation); any other ending runs the tool calls it holds. (3) Stopping kills tools: Escape, and quitting the last Hal process (Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, SIGHUP: all a deliberate stop, so the turn pauses as tasks/j1/states.md says for Ctrl-C), kill the process tree of every running bash call: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 2 s (the old Hal\'s bash.ts killProcessTree, which reaches background jobs of the detached group). The killed call\'s result says it was stopped. Tests: a fake provider that never stops asking for tools pauses at the cap; max_tokens with a tool call runs nothing and errors; Escape during `sleep 30 & sleep 30` leaves no sleep alive.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['1r'],
	foldInto: ['sj', '1r', 'j1']
}
